# MFO research/results subtrees must be byte-identical across platforms.
# The Python scripts in research-mfo/ open their JSON output with
# newline="\n" (LF) for cross-platform reproducibility. Pin LF endings here
# so Windows checkouts don't autocrlf them back to CRLF, which would
# defeat the byte-stable regeneration discipline.

research-mfo/*.py    text eol=lf
research-mfo/*.md    text eol=lf
results-mfo/*.json   text eol=lf

# The MFO notebook itself is also text; LF on every platform for diff
# stability across Linux / macOS / Windows authoring.
mfo_spectral_research_notebook.md  text eol=lf

# Attested-collector framework source-tree files. The codegen mirror
# from research/attested/ → ephemerides-spectral/python/ephemerides_spectral/_research/attested/
# is byte-exact; the manifest captures SHA-256 over the mirrored bytes. If the
# source files are CRLF on a Windows working copy, codegen captures CRLF SHAs
# while git commits them as LF, and the runtime test_data_freshness manifest
# check fails on every platform that gets the LF version. Pin LF on every
# attested-source file (TOML / JSON / NDJSON) and on the adapter modules to
# keep regenerate.py byte-stable regardless of authoring platform.
research/attested/**/*.toml          text eol=lf
research/attested/**/*.json          text eol=lf
research/attested/**/*.ndjson        text eol=lf
research/attested_adapters/*.py      text eol=lf
research/attested_collector_*.py     text eol=lf

# Top-level research/*.py — hand-coded v0.24.x catalogue data
# modules (loki_patera_data.py, saturn_rings_data.py, etc.). Pin
# LF so codegen-time SHA capture matches what git stores; otherwise
# the test_data_freshness manifest check fails on Windows authoring.
research/*.py                        text eol=lf
